<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Raymond FitzGerald</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 1185</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Raymond le Gros · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fitzgerald-dynasty">FitzGerald dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Raymond FitzGerald, called le Gros, was a Cambro-Norman knight who took part in the English invasion of Ireland. Sent ahead by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-de-clare-2nd-earl-of-pembroke">Strongbow</a> in 1170, he landed at Baginbun and defeated a force from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/waterford">Waterford</a>, and he joined Strongbow in storming the town that August. He married Strongbow's sister Basilia and served as one of the leading commanders of the Anglo-Norman settlers in Leinster.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/raymond-fitzgerald.jpg" alt="Raymond FitzGerald" width="320" height="657" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown artist of the 13th century · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:National_Library_of_Ireland_MS_700_f77v_Raymond_de_Gros.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Raymond, also called Redmond, grew up in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>. He was the son of William FitzGerald, Lord of Carew, and a grandson of Princess Nest ferch Rhys, daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhys-ap-tewdwr">Rhys ap Tewdwr</a>, the last independent Prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-wales">South Wales</a>. His cousin Gerald of Wales described him as stout, active and careful of his troops, going the rounds of the guards himself at night, and wrote that prudence was his highest quality even in war.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>At Baginbun Head on the Hook Peninsula his entrenched force was besieged by the Irish and Ostmen. Gerald of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> gives the odds as about 100 men, 10 of them knights, against 3,000; Raymond's men drove a herd of foraged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> into the enemy ranks, and about 1,000 of them were killed or captured. As <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-de-clare-2nd-earl-of-pembroke">Strongbow</a>'s second in command he had the chief share in the capture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/waterford">Waterford</a> and the assault on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a>, and in July 1171 he led a sally from Dublin after taking Strongbow's conquests to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-ii-of-england">Henry II of England</a> in Aquitaine.</p><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-de-clare-2nd-earl-of-pembroke">Strongbow</a> refused him his sister Basilia de Clare, the widow of Robert de Quincy, Raymond withdrew to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> and Hervey de Mountmaurice became constable. During the rebellion of 1174 he returned with about 450 men on the promise of the marriage, relieved Strongbow at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/waterford">Waterford</a> and married Basilia at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wexford">Wexford</a>. Reinstated as constable, he took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/limerick">Limerick</a> in October 1175. After Strongbow's death he governed until William FitzAldelm arrived, lost his estates near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a> and Wexford, and in 1182 relieved his half-uncle Robert Fitz-Stephen, besieged in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cork">Cork</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>He retired to his estates in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-waterford">County Waterford</a>. The time of his death is not certain, but he was alive when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-king-of-england">John of England</a> came to Ireland in 1185, and according to tradition he was buried in Molana Abbey. He is regarded as the soldier of the conquest, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-de-clare-2nd-earl-of-pembroke">Strongbow</a> was its statesman, and a song of the period praises him as a brave and victorious baron and constable of Leinster.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Raymond FitzGerald” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1170 Strongbow and <b>Raymond le Gros</b> stormed the town, and Strongbow married Aoife, daughter of Diarmait Mac Murchada, there.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-waterford">Kingdom of Waterford</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-waterford">County Waterford</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/ireland">Ireland</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=52.35,-3.63&amp;year=1175" aria-label="Wales on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Raymond FitzGerald"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4810 5647h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4810 5647h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=52.35,-3.63&amp;year=1175">What was here in 1175? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
